question_text:
Consider the following piece of C programming code:

int x=128,y=110;
do {
    if(x>y) x=x-y;
    else y=y-x;
} while(x!=y)
printf("%d",x);

Which one will be the output?

What will be the output of the following C programming code:

int i, j;
for (i = 1; i<5; i+=2)
    for (j=1; j<i; j+=2)
        printf("%d", j);

What will be the output of the following C Programming code:

void main()
{
    int *i, a = 12, b = 2, c;
    c = (a = a + b, b = a / b, a = a * b, b = a - b);
    i = &c;
    printf("%d", -(*i));
}

explanation:
To convert the given TAC to high-level code:

**Analysis:**
- Lines 100-102: Initializations → init = x/(y+2); limit=10;
- Lines 103-106: while condition → (i > limit && num > b)
- Lines 107-110: Loop body → num=limit-1; b=y+2; i=i-num;
- Lines 111-112: Exit → j=i-num; k=j+b;

**Optimizations:**
- t1 = y+2 and b = y+2 are redundant → b = t1
- limit = 10 is constant

**Correct high-level code:**
```
init = x/(y+2);
limit = 10;
while(i > limit && num > b) {
    num = limit - 1;
    b = y + 2;
    i = i - num;
}
j = i - num;
k = j + b;
```

Option (d) matches this exactly.
